WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.211
One of humanity's biggest obsessions
is trying to figure out

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:07.090
how cities are going to look like
in the future by the year 2050.

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:11.594
68% of the world's population is projected
to live in urban areas,

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:14.764
but most cities are not prepared for this.

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:18.143
We have congestion, crime,
a housing crisis,

00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:21.563
and just an endless array of problems
that come up when you try

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:24.691
to pack a massive amount of people
in a small footprint.

00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:26.359
Science fiction envisions

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.446
a world that's adapted to these conditions
through technology.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.365
These problems have either been eradicated
or worsened,

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:37.579
and some sci fi has been way off,
but someone's been kind of spot on.

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:42.042
But either way, they've
pushed the boundaries in our thinking and

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:45.503
they've given us a glimpse into the future
and how we could be living,

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:48.965
how we could be interacting
with our environments and with each other.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:51.885
So today

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:56.014
I want to look at three sci fi movies
with really interesting city concepts

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:59.476
and break them down from a design
and urban planning point of view.

00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:02.896
This video is sponsored
and brainstormed with Milanote

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:12.697
More on them later in this video.

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.242
The movie is based on a future world

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:20.997
Crime level has gotten so bad
that it's gone into a perpetual state

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:21.915
of martial law.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.877
The police have dual function as judges
so they can

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:30.006
give you a sentence on the spot,
including the death penalty.

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:33.676
The setting mega-city
one is a giant megalopolis

00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:35.762
that spans most of eastern U.S.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:40.308
and some of Canada, which, by the way,
is really close to the East Coast.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:44.229
Mega region in real life
and the architecture of the city

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:48.399
actually acts as a form of surveillance
in controlling the people.

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.486
People live in these really dense
residential towers with these

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:52.904
nice big atriums,

00:01:52.904 --> 00:01:56.908
but when there's a crime in the building,
it automatically triggers

00:01:56.908 --> 00:02:00.995
a system that locks you in and keeps
you contained in the building.

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:05.667
The real building, Ponte Tower, which is a residential tower near Johannesburg.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.462
That tower has eerie parallels
to the one in the movie

00:02:09.587 --> 00:02:11.506
when it was first built in the seventies.

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:16.386
It was a really desirable address,
but very quickly turned into a slum.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.806
It was hijacked by gangs at some point,
and now

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:23.351
it's like a symbol for crime and urban
decay in the city.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:27.355
The decline was largely due to the social
and political conditions

00:02:27.355 --> 00:02:31.276
at the time, but architecturally
speaking, it's brutalist.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:34.612
You know, there's a lot of similarities
with the Panopticon concept,

00:02:34.779 --> 00:02:37.490
which is the universal design
concept for prisons.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.034
And I think it's this
imposing architecture

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:42.579
that turned this building
into a symbol of decay.

00:02:42.704 --> 00:02:46.249
You know, architecture
is not just for living in or working in.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:47.709
It's also a symbol.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:52.589
And the movie actually uses these towers
as a symbol of order and surveillance.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:59.679
Like a lot of real city designs, one
of the best examples would be this guy.

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:03.975
His ideas for cities
inspired a lot of developments throughout

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:07.979
the world, and it's had a huge impact
on how cities are designed today.

00:03:08.062 --> 00:03:12.233
In 1920s,
Le Corbusier created several city plans

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:15.403
to heal Paris from its problems.

00:03:15.445 --> 00:03:16.696
It was Congest it.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.781
It was dirty, it was deteriorating.

00:03:18.781 --> 00:03:24.954
And his solution was to erase
the entire city and replace it with this.

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:28.833
He believed this would create
order and harmony by separating

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:31.920
all of the different functions
within a city like industrial,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.132
commercial, residential, recreational,
and all the different traffic arteries.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:38.259
They would all be set up in different
zones.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.429
Open space was a priority
for air circulation

00:03:41.429 --> 00:03:43.765
because it was so lacking in Paris.

00:03:43.765 --> 00:03:47.977
So he designed these blocks to have tons
of open space and circulation space

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:52.065
by packing more density
into a smaller footprint.

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:55.026
This was never really implemented
in Paris.

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:59.781
Thank God, but many housing projects
throughout the world took this approach

00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:03.159
and they actually became
kind of awful places to live.

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:07.288
If you separate all of these functions,
you get dead zones throughout the day.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:08.915
You go to work during the day.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.750
You come back home during the evening.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.128
And if you have to work late,
walking in this office area

00:04:14.128 --> 00:04:17.257
becomes kind of dangerous
because the streets are dead.

00:04:17.465 --> 00:04:19.050
Same with the residential areas.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.053
During certain hours,
there's like nobody on the streets.

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:25.223
All the activity, like restaurants
or bar hours

00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:28.393
that would be open
late are in a separate zone.

00:04:28.476 --> 00:04:31.521
And that's why well-designed cities.

00:04:31.562 --> 00:04:33.439
There's a mixture of different uses.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:36.442
There's shops,
restaurants, offices and apartments.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:40.655
So you can get a constant flow of activity
and eyes on the streets.

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:44.617
Cities and neighborhoods
become a lot safer and more lively.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:47.161
When you have
this kind of organic surveillance.

00:04:47.161 --> 00:04:49.038
But in Corbusier's designs,

00:04:49.038 --> 00:04:53.710
this was never really considered,
which created really unsafe areas.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.004
And the need for constant surveillance.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.216
So in a future where crime rate is through
the roof, it's

00:05:00.216 --> 00:05:04.178
not really an accident that they use
this type of city design in this movie.

00:05:08.558 --> 00:05:11.978
Whenever I make a video like this,
it takes up a lot of research

