00:06:29.472 --> 00:06:31.724
The movie The Fifth Element depicts

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:35.603
a megacity that solved the population
crisis by building vertically.

00:06:35.645 --> 00:06:40.566
These towers go on for miles
and miles and miles and they use flying

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:44.946
cars and public transit, navigating
in three dimensions to get around.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:49.700
Natural light and ventilation is an issue,
and the further you go down,

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:50.952
it gets worse and worse.

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:54.872
Even above, it's
not really the nicest conditions.

00:06:55.581 --> 00:07:00.586
People live in these little pods
with like little to no personalization.

00:07:00.711 --> 00:07:03.881
Building vertically makes a lot of sense,
especially if it's a city

00:07:03.881 --> 00:07:05.174
with a clear perimeter.

00:07:05.174 --> 00:07:08.386
There's a lot of efficiencies
when your infrastructure can serve

00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:10.346
more people in a smaller footprint.

00:07:10.346 --> 00:07:12.056
There's a couple of technologies

00:07:12.056 --> 00:07:14.934
that would often evolve
to make this scenario plausible.

00:07:15.059 --> 00:07:18.521
You would need flying cars
or some sort of vertical transportation,

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.607
which is actually not that far fetched.

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:24.610
They're already testing these automated
taxi drones.

00:07:24.694 --> 00:07:28.990
We would also need new materials
and new construction technology.

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:31.993
Like, for example,
there's this new type of bio concrete.

00:07:31.993 --> 00:07:33.786
I can heal its own cracks.

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:37.915
There's also been a lot of development and
heavy timber construction, for example.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:41.461
We have the tallest wood tower
being built in Japan,

00:07:41.502 --> 00:07:45.256
which is obviously huge because you can
offset a lot of the carbon footprint.

00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:49.594
I think more than the material strength,
it's the construct ability

00:07:49.594 --> 00:07:51.012
that's going to be a bigger issue.

00:07:51.012 --> 00:07:53.097
How do you get materials that high?

00:07:53.097 --> 00:07:56.851
It's fine when it's like 30 or 40 storeys,
but when it goes up to

00:07:56.851 --> 00:08:01.147
like 100 storeys,
the concrete starts to harden.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:04.609
And that's problematic.

00:08:07.236 --> 00:08:10.448
You know, Burj Khalifa,
the tallest building in the world.

00:08:10.448 --> 00:08:14.452
They created a special mixture
that can travel in liquid form

00:08:14.452 --> 00:08:15.745
over a long distance.

00:08:15.745 --> 00:08:17.079
And they're going to keep coming up

00:08:17.079 --> 00:08:19.499
with these new solutions
for these new challenges.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:22.668
But maybe the construction doesn't
even happen at ground level.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:27.340
Maybe it starts at an intermediate level
that acts as another ground plane.

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:32.512
In fact, you can see a lot of bridges
connecting different buildings.

00:08:32.553 --> 00:08:36.390
If you want to go from here to here,
it doesn't really make sense for you

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.435
to have to go all the way down
to get to another building.

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:43.397
And we actually do have cities,
mostly colder,

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:47.360
windy cities like Minneapolis or Calgary
that have smaller

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.446
versions of these bridges,
and they're called skyways.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:53.282
People park their cars, they go to work,

00:08:53.574 --> 00:08:57.370
they go shopping, and they can do
all of this without going outside.

00:08:57.453 --> 00:09:00.790
One of the reasons I moved away from
Toronto was because of the cold.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.044
So I really, truly
appreciate these internal pathways, but

00:09:05.169 --> 00:09:09.674
it's kind of a problem
because not everyone gets to use them.

00:09:09.799 --> 00:09:14.428
These skyways are privately owned
because they connect private buildings.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.475
So the developers don't really have
an incentive to make them more welcoming.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.271
In fact, when the city of Minneapolis
hired architectural consultants

00:09:23.271 --> 00:09:26.232
to improve the accessibility
to the skyways,

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:29.402
most of the developers response was

00:09:29.694 --> 00:09:33.489
that they don't really want to attract
the wrong kind

00:09:33.489 --> 00:09:37.952
of people in an entire city
that's connected with skyways.

00:09:37.994 --> 00:09:43.833
It really makes me wonder where
is the line between public and private?

00:09:46.502 --> 00:09:49.005
Let's talk about the constructability
again for a sec.

00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:50.798
A lot of people think that the problem
with tall

00:09:50.798 --> 00:09:53.050
buildings is the weight of the building,

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:57.555
but actually the bigger issue
is the force of the wind on the building.

00:09:57.638 --> 00:09:59.849
We call this the lateral force.

00:09:59.974 --> 00:10:03.603
Basically, the higher you go, the stronger
the wind force

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.229
is going to be on the building.

00:10:05.229 --> 00:10:08.149
Like if you have a wind force
of ten kilometers

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:12.320
an hour at the bottom of a building,
it can go up to like 70 or 80 on top.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.574
And apparently at the top of the Burj
Khalifa, it's like 200 kilometers an hour.

00:10:16.657 --> 00:10:19.076
And it's also really unpredictable.

00:10:19.118 --> 00:10:22.455
A common concept when it comes to tall
buildings is torquing,

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:24.665
not twerking, torquing.

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:26.500
It's like twisting.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.378
One of the solutions is exterior bracing.

00:10:29.503 --> 00:10:33.299
It holds the building in place
and it stops it from twisting.

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:36.677
A lot of these buildings
will probably look more like this.

00:10:36.802 --> 00:10:38.596
Another solution is

00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:42.224
the original concept is from Frank Lloyd
Wright in his mile high tower.

00:10:42.642 --> 00:10:45.519
It's dense at the bottom
and it tapers at the top.

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:48.189
When it was built,
it was pretty revolutionary.

00:10:48.189 --> 00:10:53.527
But now you have the Burj Khalifa and
other skyscrapers using the same concept.

00:10:53.736 --> 00:10:54.820
But the problem with this

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.366
is that you need to make the bottom
so fat to get a smaller top,

00:10:58.366 --> 00:11:03.079
and it's just not really economical
to drop off so much mass at the top.

00:11:03.162 --> 00:11:07.500
So one of the new solutions
is by Calatrava in Dubai,

00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:10.753
and it's going to be
the tallest tower in the world.

00:11:10.753 --> 00:11:13.464
And I think it's kind of genius.

00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:17.385
Instead of bracing it or bundling it,
they've got cables

00:11:17.385 --> 00:11:20.971
attached throughout the building
to hold it when it sways.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.141
So, for example,
when the building wants to sway this

00:11:24.141 --> 00:11:27.603
way, it'll have cables
pulling from this side.

00:11:27.812 --> 00:11:30.731
And if the building wants to sway
this way, it'll have cables

00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:32.108
pulling from the side.

00:11:32.108 --> 00:11:33.818
By the way,
if you guys want more information

00:11:33.818 --> 00:11:36.445
on these structural kits,
I'll leave the link in the description.

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:39.615
I mean, just a couple hundred years ago,
people would have never thought

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:40.658
of skyscrapers.

00:11:40.658 --> 00:11:44.078
You know, we only started getting these
since the invention of elevators.

00:11:44.161 --> 00:11:48.457
So probably in the future,
with new materials and new technology,

00:11:48.457 --> 00:11:54.004
we're probably going to be able to get
like 500 stories or a thousand stories.

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:58.050
I think the bigger problem is going to be
how can we create equitable

00:11:58.050 --> 00:11:59.427
spaces throughout?

00:11:59.427 --> 00:12:03.055
You know, like I said, there's
this blurring of the private

00:12:03.055 --> 00:12:07.059
and public spaces,
but also having this height naturally

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:10.396
creates
the really desirable spaces at the top

00:12:10.396 --> 00:12:13.149
and the really undesirable section
at the bottom.

00:12:13.357 --> 00:12:17.903
We would need artificial lighting
that really closely mimics daylight

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:22.450
and also a way to provide
fresh air for the lower areas.

00:12:22.658 --> 00:12:27.204
But also just making sure
that we have the right policies in place

00:12:27.204 --> 00:12:38.215
to not create these social divides
between these sections.

00:12:42.011 --> 00:12:44.096
It doesn't
really matter how tall you can build

00:12:44.221 --> 00:12:47.349
if there isn't a way to move around
efficiently.

00:12:47.558 --> 00:12:52.271
Minority Report has a much
more comprehensive vision of how a city

00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:57.193
could completely reshape itself
based on this new transportation system.

00:12:57.318 --> 00:13:01.030
Instead of flying cars,
they have these maglev pods

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:06.535
that travel on these dedicated lanes which
are integrated into the urban fabric.

00:13:06.535 --> 00:13:09.914
And they even become
a part of the building's facade.

00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:13.375
The design of these is actually really
cool.

00:13:13.417 --> 00:13:16.670
It's got an internal gyroscope
so that you can stay upright,

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.465
whether you're traveling
horizontally or vertically.

00:13:19.632 --> 00:13:21.217
It's self-driving.

00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:23.594
It's controlled by a centralized A.I..

00:13:23.677 --> 00:13:26.806
So if you trust the government,
you just hop

00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:29.934
in, put in your destination, and boom,
you're there.

00:13:29.975 --> 00:13:34.146
It takes out the human error factor
so there's less chances of collisions,

00:13:34.146 --> 00:13:38.984
and they can maneuver in a way that can
solve a lot of these congestion issues.

00:13:39.026 --> 00:13:42.905
Now, this type of infrastructure
is kind of

00:13:42.905 --> 00:13:45.533
a similar idea
to the one in fifth element.

00:13:45.908 --> 00:13:49.161
But the biggest difference
is that you can go straight

00:13:49.161 --> 00:13:53.916
from your destination to your apartment
and you don't need to go outside at all.

00:13:54.041 --> 00:13:57.545
And I think that would change
the social dynamics in so many ways.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.714
There's
a kind of forced social interaction

00:14:00.714 --> 00:14:03.884
that happens just by the fact
that you live close to someone

00:14:03.926 --> 00:14:08.597
and you run into each other in lobbies
or in the elevators.

00:14:08.597 --> 00:14:12.560
And if these lobbies or amenity
spaces are designed in a way

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:17.231
that encourages social interaction,
then you get to know your neighbors.

00:14:17.356 --> 00:14:20.442
So even if you don't have any friends
or family,

00:14:20.568 --> 00:14:24.071
at least you have the people around you
that you can interact with.

00:14:24.154 --> 00:14:27.241
That's the traditional idea of a community

00:14:27.366 --> 00:14:31.036
and having neighbors
with more and more towers being built.

00:14:31.078 --> 00:14:34.498
This idea of community has been slowly
and slowly

00:14:34.498 --> 00:14:37.418
disintegrating,
which adds to the social isolation.

00:14:37.793 --> 00:14:42.965
But maybe a system like this
could actually democratize buildings.

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:48.095
These days we have a lot of sky gardens
and amenity spaces within buildings,

00:14:48.095 --> 00:14:51.265
but they can only be used
through the building.

00:14:51.307 --> 00:14:55.311
So by having the ability to access
the building from different points.

00:14:55.311 --> 00:14:57.062
Maybe some of these spaces

00:14:57.062 --> 00:15:01.650
could be made accessible to the public
or to paid members through an app.

00:15:01.775 --> 00:15:05.529
All of this is really exciting,
but who knows?

00:15:05.529 --> 00:15:09.992
Maybe in the future our built environments
are just going to be secondary

00:15:09.992 --> 00:15:12.995
and we're going to be spending more time
in the virtual reality.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:16.790
Or maybe cities become cool organisms

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.877
to protect against natural disasters
or a threat of war.

00:15:20.002 --> 00:15:25.049
Or maybe we just stop trying
to improve the city altogether.

00:15:25.174 --> 00:15:29.261
Mark Wigley once said architecture
never derived its force

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:32.723
from stability of culture,
but rather from the expression

00:15:32.723 --> 00:15:35.726
of those moments
when the sense of stability slipped.

00:15:35.851 --> 00:15:39.521
If we can agree on one thing,
I think it's that we're living

00:15:39.521 --> 00:15:42.942
in really uncertain times,
and this is the breeding ground

00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:45.277
for a lot of the changes
in the environment.

00:15:45.402 --> 00:15:47.029
There's a lot to unpack here,

00:15:47.029 --> 00:15:49.406
so let me know what you think
in the comments.

00:15:49.406 --> 00:15:52.159
And if you want to know more
about architecture,

00:15:52.159 --> 00:15:54.536
I'm going to leave a playlist
at the end of this.

00:15:54.578 --> 00:15:57.164
And if you like sci fi
or dystopian architecture,

00:15:57.206 --> 00:16:00.751
you should probably have a look at my Neom
and Burning Man videos.

00:16:00.834 --> 00:16:04.088
Don't forget to like and subscribe
and I'll see you guys in

00:16:04.088 --> 00:16:06.767
the next video.

